## Migration: BackgroundProcessor → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort` in Stop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue execution after abort | Check `IsCancellationRequested` and continue |
| `GetNextItem()` blocking | Blocking call interruption | Async with token or `WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Grace period | `Task.Delay` or `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same logic that was in the
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to loop exit
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't exit in time. In modern .NET there is NO way to
            // forcibly kill it — the code must cooperate. Log a warning.
            Console.WriteLine("Warning: worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept and honor the token:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — blocking with no cancellation
private Item GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks forever if empty
}

// After (.NET 8) — unblocks on cancellation
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
}
```

If it uses `Thread.Sleep` internally, replace with:
```csharp
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollIntervalMs); // wakes immediately on cancel
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `GetNextItem()` will block forever — cancellation never fires. The token must reach the actual wait point. |
| **No forcible kill exists** | There is no `.NET 8` equivalent of `Thread.Abort`. If code doesn't cooperate, it won't stop. Design checkpoints accordingly. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Always `Dispose()` it. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid catching unrelated cancellations. |
| **Assuming immediate cancellation** | Cancellation only fires at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` runs for minutes without checking the token, cancellation is delayed. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long operations. |

### If `ProcessItem` is Long-Running

Add checkpoints inside it:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(Item item, CancellationToken token)
{
    foreach (var step in item.Steps)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Execute(step);
    }
}
```

### Summary

The fundamental shift: **.NET Framework let you kill threads externally**; **.NET 8 requires threads to volunteer to stop**. Every blocking or long-running method in the call chain must accept and check the `CancellationToken`, or your `Stop()` will hang at the `Join`.
